<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- The full-screen player's play/pause disc. Phone only - fragment_player_tv.xml and
     fragment_player_mini_tv.xml keep ripple_68dp and are deliberately untouched.
     64dp DISC INSIDE THE EXISTING 72dp BOX, via a 4dp inset rather than by resizing the view. The
     box stays 72dp because PlayerFragment measures item_player_controls into
     uiViewModel.playerControlsHeight, which PlayerTrackAdapter writes onto item_player_track.xml's
     player_controls_placeholder, which is what the collapse morph's toMoveY is computed from.
     Changing the box moves the morph; insetting the drawing does not - a drawable inset has no
     effect on View measurement at all. The touch target also stays 72dp, which is the other reason
     not to shrink the view.
     ⚠⚠ [CORRECTED 2026-10-16] THIS WAS 56dp, AND THE REASON GIVEN FOR 56 WAS WRONG.
     The old note said "indicatorSize=64dp, so the ring occupies roughly 60-64dp and the disc stops
     at 56dp - a 4dp gap each side". Decoded from the 1.14.0 AAR instead of estimated:
     CircularDrawingDelegate.getSize() is `indicatorSize + 2 * indicatorInset`, indicatorInset
     defaults to 4dp (mtrl_progress_circular_inset_medium), so at indicatorSize=64dp the preferred
     size was 64 + 8 = 72dp - exactly the view, scale 1.0 - and the ring occupied diameter 56 to
     64dp with a 4dp track. The disc's edge and the ring's INNER edge therefore coincided exactly:
     the "4dp gap" was 0dp, which is part of why the hero read as flat against the 48dp satellites
     (56/48 = 1.17x, an 8dp step).
     ⚠⚠ SO THE RING MOVED OUTSIDE THE DISC RATHER THAN THE DISC STAYING SMALL. The
     layout now sets indicatorSize=72dp AND indicatorInset=0dp, giving getSize() = 72 = the view,
     scale still 1.0, ring at diameter 64 to 72dp - immediately outside this 64dp disc, in the 8dp
     of the box the disc does not use. Both numbers are load-bearing together: raise indicatorSize
     without zeroing the inset and getSize() becomes 80, the delegate scales by 72/80 and the ring
     lands back on top of the disc.
     ⚠️ WHY THE RING MATTERS ENOUGH TO PAY FOR: trackPlayPause is the LATER child in the
     FrameLayout, so this disc paints OVER the indicator - a 64dp disc with the old 64dp ring would
     have hidden the buffering spinner completely, and that spinner is the LAST buffering affordance
     left in the app (the full-screen and TV seek bars lost theirs in 1055; see the bufferBar note
     in item_player_controls.xml).
     FILL IS ?attr/colorPrimary, NOT PlayerColors.accent, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE WHOLE CONTROL.
     accent is a raw Palette swatch (PlayerColors.getColorsFrom: lightVibrantSwatch ?: vibrantSwatch
     ?: lightMutedSwatch, else titleTextColor) with NO paired on-colour anywhere in PlayerColors, and
     color/button_player_heart.xml already records what that costs: accent is acceptable on a glyph
     "precisely because a muted accent makes the heart SUBTLE, never illegible - unlike a container
     fill, where the same fallback would erase the element." A 64dp disc is exactly that container
     fill. ?attr/colorPrimary comes with ?attr/colorOnPrimary guaranteed to contrast with it, and
     under isPlayerColor() + isDynamic() the theme has ALREADY been re-seeded from the artwork accent
     (PlayerFragment's playerColors observer recreates the activity for it), so the disc is still
     artwork-derived in that mode - with an on-colour, which accent cannot supply.
     Theme attributes in drawable XML resolve against the inflating view's theme from API 21; minSdk
     is 24. -->
<ripple xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:color="@color/ripple_player_on_primary"
    android:radius="32dp">
    <item>
        <inset android:inset="4dp">
            <shape android:shape="oval">
                <solid android:color="?attr/colorPrimary" />
            </shape>
        </inset>
    </item>
</ripple>
